\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{Square counts on twenty vertices}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
We reuse the prescribed-cycle search of~\cite{latin}, adding two
reductions whose proofs apply to every stage of that search. They
allow us to recover three further assertions from Table~4 of
Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}. The classification below uses exact
finite computation; its completeness does not follow from the
number of examples in the source.

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
Up to isomorphism, there are exactly two simple graphs on twenty
vertices, of degree six and girth four, with nine squares through
each edge, and exactly five with ten squares through each edge.
They are the seven explicit graphs in the accompanying certificate.
Moreover $n(6,4,7)\ge24$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}\label{bip}
If a bipartite $k$-regular graph with parts of size $m\ge2$ has constant
square count $\lambda$, then
\[
 \lambda\ge\frac{k(k-1)^2}{m-1}-(k-1).
\]
Consequently a bipartite graph of degree six and order twenty
has constant square count at least twelve.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Fix $x$ in one part. For each other vertex $y$ in that part put
$c_y=|N(x)\cap N(y)|$. Counting paths of length two from $x$
gives $\sum c_y=k(k-1)$. Counting squares through $x$ gives
$\sum c_y(c_y-1)=k\lambda$: the left side counts each square
twice, and the squares through $x$ number $k\lambda/2$.
Cauchy--Schwarz yields
\[
 k\lambda+k(k-1)=\sum c_y^2
       \ge \frac{k^2(k-1)^2}{m-1}.
\]
This is the asserted bound. Substitution of $k=6,m=10$ gives
$\lambda\ge35/3$, hence $\lambda\ge12$.
\end{proof}

\section*{The common finite search}
The acquired shortest-odd-cycle inequality $k\ell\le2n$ forces
a five-cycle in every nonbipartite case of Theorem~\ref{main}.
Indeed $6\ell\le40$, whereas an odd cycle longer than five has
length at least seven. Use the cycle classes from~\cite{latin}:
$a_i$ vertices have the two cycle neighbours $v_{i-1},v_{i+1}$,
$b_i$ have the single cycle neighbour $v_i$, and $z$ have none.
The cycle vertices themselves are included in the $a_i$. Here
\[
 a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}+b_i=6,\qquad
 \sum_i b_i+2z=10,\qquad a_i\ge1,\quad b_i,z\ge0.
                                                               \tag{1}
\]
The same weak-composition enumeration and inverse linear formulas
as in~\cite{latin} give $407$ labelled patterns, or $55$ patterns
under the ten rotations and reversals of the specified cycle.
Enumerating all positive $a$-vectors independently gives the same
list. Distinct labels are assigned to all vertices in each pattern;
no automorphism of a completed graph is postulated.

Recall the acquired recursion. All edges incident with the cycle
are inserted first. At a partial graph, a set $U$ of active vertices
has unassigned remaining neighbours; every vertex outside $U$ has
its full degree. Choose one $x\in U$, enumerate admissible subsets
of its active candidate neighbours of size equal to its deficit,
insert those edges and remove $x$ from $U$. Triangle and degree
obstructions are checked as in the acquired complete proof.

\begin{lemma}\label{cells}
At every node of this recursion, $U$ is independent in the partial
graph. Among the candidate neighbours of the chosen $x$, partition
vertices by their exact current neighbourhood. It suffices to
choose a number from each part and take the first that many vertices
of the part in the fixed label order. This retains a representative
of every possible completion up to isomorphism.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Initially no edge between active vertices has been inserted.
A recursive step adds edges only from $x$ and then removes $x$
from the active set. This proves independence by induction.
The current neighbours of each active vertex are therefore all
closed vertices.

Two candidates with the same current neighbourhood have the same
deficit, because the required degree is fixed. They have the same
original cycle neighbours as well, since all cycle vertices are
closed. Their initial allowed relations to every other vertex
consequently agree: that relation forbids exactly a common cycle
neighbour. They also have identical adjacency to every closed
vertex and no adjacency within $U$.

Thus every permutation within each indicated part, fixing $x$
and all other vertices, preserves the entire partial problem:
present edges, original allowed pairs, degree deficits, closed
vertices and the constant-square target. The symmetric groups on
the parts act independently. Their orbits on neighbour subsets
are specified exactly by the number chosen in each part. Taking
the first prescribed number supplies one representative of each
orbit, including zero choices and a zero deficit.

For any valid completion, transport its next neighbour subset to
this representative by the corresponding permutation. The transported
graph is another completion of the same partial problem and survives
that branch. Apply induction at the resulting child. The later
vertex-selection rule need not commute with the permutation: the
induction applies to whichever labelled child was reached.
This proves coverage of isomorphism classes. It neither counts
labelled completions nor ensures a single representative per class.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{closed}
The square-count interval used for an initial cycle edge in
\cite{latin} is valid for every edge whose endpoints are closed.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $xy$ have both endpoints outside $U$. The sets
$L=N(x)\setminus\{y\}$ and $R=N(y)\setminus\{x\}$ are already
final. Triangle-freeness makes them disjoint. Every square through
$xy$ corresponds to exactly one edge from $L$ to $R$.
The present cross edges therefore give a lower bound $q$.
Every missing cross edge in a completion has both endpoints
active: no closed vertex can acquire another edge. Its endpoints
must still pass the acquired candidate test, since existing common
neighbours and exhausted degrees cannot later disappear.

Let $p$ count all such currently possible missing cross edges.
Then the final square count lies in $[q,q+p]$. Some counted edges
may be mutually incompatible; this only increases the upper bound.
Retain a target precisely when it lies in these intervals for all
closed edges. A valid completion with that constant target always
survives. At a leaf all edges are checked explicitly as before.
\end{proof}

The new implementation replaces subset enumeration by the integer
choices of Lemma~\ref{cells}, and applies Lemma~\ref{closed} to
all closed edges. Every recursive return restores both adjacency
and deficits. The unsigned masks contain all twenty vertices.
These are the only mathematical changes to the previously reviewed
search. Time limits can return an incomplete status but never an
exclusion. Every retained graph is exported in full; no storage cap
can discard a positive result.

\section*{The finite result and its verification}
The certificate completes all $55$ patterns for targets $7,8,9,10$,
visiting $8\,626\,624$ nodes and retaining $86$ labelled outputs.
Different outputs may represent the same graph; the count is not an
isomorphism count.
No retained graph has count seven or eight. For counts nine and ten,
every retained adjacency is supplied with a literal permutation to
one of the following seven source examples. The verifier checks all
$20^2$ adjacency comparisons for each permutation. Discovery of the
permutations used a graph-isomorphism program; their verification
uses only the displayed integer permutations.

The examples are independently checked for degree six, absence of
triangles and their exact square count on every edge. A triangle-free
graph of degree six has at least twelve vertices in each component,
so a graph here is connected. In the table, $A$ denotes adjacency
and $t$ is the number of singleton classes under equality of open
neighbourhoods. These invariants distinguish every pair with the
same square count.
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{c|r|r|r|r}
Source example&$\lambda$&$\operatorname{tr}(A^5)$&$\operatorname{tr}(A^{10})$&$t$\\\hline
84&10&2440&74996040&20\\
85&10&2430&74996010&20\\
86&10&2440&74996000&20\\
87&10&2420&74996040&20\\
88&10&2400&74996040&20\\
89&9&3840&64665600&8\\
90&9&3840&64665600&0
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The source identifiers are the one-based lines of its fixed acquired
\texttt{UntilOrder250} graph file. The literal encodings and their
hashes accompany the certificate. The traces are recomputed by
integer matrix multiplication, and equality of neighbourhoods is
checked on the actual adjacency rows. No isomorphism-classification
library is a premise of the nonisomorphism test.

Lemma~\ref{bip} excludes the bipartite alternative. Equations~(1),
the acquired complete branching proof and Lemmas~\ref{cells}--\ref{closed}
prove completeness in the other alternative. The verified maps give
at most two or five classes; the seven verified, distinguished
examples give the corresponding lower counts. This proves the two
classification assertions of Theorem~\ref{main}.

For the remaining lower bound, the acquired general tree bound gives
$n\ge12+\lceil2(25-7)/6\rceil=18$. Counting edge--square incidences
gives $8\mid6n\cdot7$, hence $4\mid n$. The only possible order
below twenty-four is therefore twenty, which has been excluded.
This proves $n(6,4,7)\ge24$. The separate exclusion for count eight
at twenty is recorded but does not by itself prove the source's
lower bound twenty-two.

\paragraph{Attribution and limits.}
All three headline conclusions are prior entries in Table~4 of
\cite{GJ}, physical page~20. The positive examples remain attributed
to the acquired source data. The tree bound is the original bound
of Drglin, Filipovski, Jajcay and Raiman, acquired with its complete
proof through the preceding lower-bounds component. The fixed-cycle
partition, initial degree completion and square interval are acquired
from~\cite{latin}; its underlying recursion and C++ translation are
attributed in the preceding components. The present work supplies
the interchangeable-vertex reduction, the use of every closed edge,
and their application to these finite classifications. No general
historical priority for these elementary methods is claimed.
No open conjecture, whole F012 recovery, Lean theorem or compression
measurement is claimed here.

